## Configuration and directives

A builder definition describes a constrained source surface. Rust helpers make
that description convenient while Moth owns parsing, typing, folding and the
retained facts consumed by later stages.

### Describe builder configuration

Use the configuration helper for builder-owned settings:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    let definition = BuilderDefinition::new("game")
        .config("game", |config| {
            config.string("asset_root").default("assets");
            config.bool("debug_overlay").default(false);
        })
        .finish()?;
]

The closure constructs a schema. It isn't a callback executed during Moth
expression evaluation. The helper records field names, value types, required
or optional status and defaults in the normal configuration contract.
`finish` validates the definition. Authored values then use the compiler's
configuration service and produce typed builder settings.

A default satisfies an omitted permitted value. It doesn't replace malformed
input or an explicitly supplied value. Missing required values, unknown fields,
duplicate names and invalid value domains are errors. Optionality and omission
are separate rules rather than an instruction to deep-merge records.

`game` names a builder-owned configuration contract. This helper doesn't add
arbitrary fields to the compiler-owned `$project` signature or reinstate a
separate configuration expression language. Configuration stays a
self-contained compile-time source. It doesn't run host functions, import
application modules or perform runtime requests.

The selected builder is settled before configuration compilation. A setting
doesn't silently select another builder or physical backend. Explicit source
input contracts, project metadata and builder-provided globals retain their
separate receiving rules. A builder global is stable semantic configuration,
not a way to expose host architecture, secrets or operating-system identity to
source evaluation.

### Add a metadata directive

Metadata directives describe facts a builder consumes. For example, a module
may declare a bundle label through a root-local directive. Its Rust definition
specifies the name, permitted context and typed argument:

[codeblock, $code("rust"):
    use moth::builder::{
        DirectiveArgument, DirectiveContext, MetadataDirective,
    };

    let bundle = MetadataDirective::new("bundle")
        .context(DirectiveContext::ModuleRoot)
        .argument(DirectiveArgument::string("name").required())
        .finish()?;

    let definition = BuilderDefinition::new("game")
        .metadata_directive(bundle)
        .finish()?;
]

The compiler recognises the directive through its registered descriptor,
validates placement, parses arguments with the shared argument owner and folds
their compile-time values. `CompiledProject` exposes the resulting metadata
through its registered metadata views, paired with authored locations and
typed argument access. The builder consumes those facts rather than scanning
source, matching reserved constant names or inspecting generated strings.

This example declares metadata only. It doesn't create an execution entry.
A root-purpose registration also needs a consumer contract that defines entry
selection, signature, lifecycle and outcome handling. A function declaration
modifier or a semantic block requires its own compiler-owned contract.
Registering a name isn't permission to invent one.

### Keep directive powers bounded

A descriptor fixes the directive's context, form, argument contract and
processing role. A builder can provide template formatting and consume
configuration, metadata and permitted root-purpose facts. It cannot use that
registration to rewrite a function, change nominal type identity, alter exports,
mutate HIR or relax borrow and lifetime validation.

The registry rejects duplicate and compiler-owned names. Unknown directives,
wrong placement and an unavailable selected capability remain distinct errors.
Ordinary static conditional code doesn't suppress frontend validation.
Structural exclusion belongs to the compiler's pre-graph feature-selection
contract, not to a builder callback or configuration Boolean.

Metadata preserves its declared scope. Project settings don't automatically
merge with root metadata. A provider's metadata isn't inherited by its
consumer. Repeated directive occurrences are accepted only when their
registered cardinality allows them.

### Register template behaviour

Use `template_directive` with a template descriptor to supply a formatter and
its permitted argument and body-mode contracts. Body modes, head compatibility,
whitespace policy and formatting effects come from the compiler's supported
vocabulary. They aren't an arbitrary lexer or grammar plugin.

The compiler owns argument typing and the structural template input. A
formatter consumes its documented view and preserves structural resource and
site-root anchors when composing output. It doesn't replace those anchors with
guessed URLs or mutate the compiler's template store. Invalid arguments produce
located diagnostics through the normal compiler path.

Template directives remain head operations. They don't make a String into a
component instance, subscription or saved renderer. A directive that generates
HTML still needs the appropriate escaping for each output context. Returning
HTML is not proof that interpolated text is safe to insert as markup.

Source-backed Moth helpers are the simpler choice when ordinary template
composition and functions already express the operation. Use a Rust formatter
only for behaviour that belongs at the formatting boundary.

### Select source adapters and external providers

`moth_templates` and `markdown` enable compiler-known source adapters. An `.mtf`
file contributes its const-required content through normal module compilation.
A `.md` file contributes rendered content. Registering a filename extension
doesn't add a new Moth declaration grammar.

Use `external_import_provider` for a foreign file format that supplies a typed
binding surface. The provider declares its stable kind and supported
extensions. It receives a resolved source request and returns typed package
facts, diagnostics and any runtime asset requirements.

Provider code can parse its own foreign format. It doesn't reparse Moth imports
or perform another module-discovery pass. Source identity, package registration,
tracked input access and diagnostic locations come from the supplied services,
not from private string or path interners.

Registration conflicts are diagnosed rather than resolved by whichever
provider runs first. Provider work records all semantic inputs needed for
invalidation. Runtime assets share ordinary resource and output ownership with
directly registered host packages. Their consumers don't reconstruct imports
by scanning emitted JavaScript, HTML or Wasm.

### Reuse the selected surface for tools

Checking and editor analysis use the same builder definition as compilation.
Tooling can add analysis and validation roots without creating a competing
package registry or output builder. A directive or binding has the same meaning
in an editor, a check and a release build.

A definition becomes immutable before source compilation. Changes to packages,
signatures, directives or relevant configuration invalidate the affected
semantic or physical work through the normal dependency facts. Runtime state,
function-pointer addresses and absolute installation paths aren't stable
semantic fingerprint keys.
